lesson:
An **event** is the browser telling your code that something happened: a click, a key, a form being sent. You've used `addEventListener("click", ...)` already. Now look closer, because most bugs in interactive pages are event bugs.

**The event object.** Your function receives one argument describing what happened:

```
button.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  console.log(event.type);           // "click"
  console.log(event.target);         // the element actually clicked
  console.log(event.currentTarget);  // the element the listener is on
});
```

**Which event?**
- `click`: buttons, links, anything clicked or tapped.
- `input`: fires on every change to a text box as you type. Good for live counters and search.
- `change`: fires when a change is *committed*: a choice in a `<select>`, a checkbox ticked, or a text box left after editing.
- `submit`: fires on a `<form>` when its submit button is pressed **or Enter is pressed** in a box. Listen on the form, not the button, and you catch both.

**Stop the reload.** A form's old default is to send the data to a server and load a new page. In an app that runs in the page, that reload wipes everything. The first line of a submit handler is almost always:

```
form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();   // stay on this page
  // ...read the boxes and do the work
});
```

**Pass the function, don't call it.** `button.addEventListener("click", update())` runs `update` once, immediately, and hands its result (usually `undefined`) to the browser. Write `update` without brackets, or wrap it: `() => update(5)`.

**One handler for many buttons.** A game shop with twenty items doesn't need twenty listeners. Put the facts on each button with data attributes, and one listener on the container:

```
<div id="shop">
  <button data-item="sword" data-cost="50">Sword</button>
  <button data-item="bow" data-cost="80">Bow</button>
</div>
```
```
shop.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const item = event.target.dataset.item;
  if (!item) return;                 // clicked the gap, not a button
  buy(item, Number(event.target.dataset.cost));
});
```

Clicks **bubble**: they start at the button and travel up through its parents, so the container's listener hears them, and `event.target` says which button it was. This is called **event delegation**. It also works for buttons added later, which matters when lists are built from data. (If a button holds an icon or a `<span>`, the target may be that inner element; `event.target.closest("button")` finds the button.)

Keep the handlers thin: read the input, call a plain function that does the real work, show the result.

example.code:
let gold = 120;
const shop = document.getElementById("shop");
const message = document.getElementById("message");

function buy(item, cost) {                 // the rule, with no DOM in it
  if (cost > gold) return `Not enough gold for ${item}`;
  gold -= cost;
  return `Bought ${item}`;
}

// ONE listener for every button inside #shop (event delegation)
shop.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const button = event.target.closest("button");
  if (!button) return;                     // a click on the gap
  message.textContent = buy(button.dataset.item, Number(button.dataset.cost));
});

const form = document.getElementById("rename");
form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();                  // no page reload
  document.getElementById("hero").textContent = document.getElementById("name").value.trim();
});

